import{_ as t,o,c as s,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.BUOFcz_J.js";const w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Browser application","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/frontend.md","filePath":"reference/frontend.md"}'),n={name:"reference/frontend.md"};function i(r,e,d,h,c,l){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="browser-application" tabindex="-1">Browser application <a class="header-anchor" href="#browser-application" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Browser application&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Updated 2026-11-11. The frontend was reset at the user&#39;s request. Previous frontend plans, visual specifications, Paper references and appearance rulers have been removed from the working repository. Git retains their history; they do not govern this implementation. This document describes the current application, not another prescriptive design system. How it changes next is decided by reviewing real runs (<a href="/Rho/docs/concepts/development#stages">Development</a>); the current <a href="/Rho/docs/records/base-r-episode#review-of-the-2026-10-25-run">base-R review</a> records what the screens do not yet show. Avoid unnecessary secondary labels and descriptions: let the layout do the explaining.</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures/demo.gif" alt="The application during a short session"></p><p>The clip runs for about 30 seconds: the projects list, the answer board with its figure and table, a script and a table opened, and panes moved and resized while the work stays in place. It was recorded before the frame below; the places and the work are the same.</p><h2 id="the-frame" tabindex="-1">The frame <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-frame" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The frame&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every page of a project has the same three regions. The sidebar says where the person is and what is going on there: the projects, then the open project&#39;s tasks, then its files. The project&#39;s places are tabs above the work, which is one panel. One panel beside the work holds the Agent&#39;s conversation or the project&#39;s notes. Each can be switched with one click from any page, and switching never changes what is running (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#one-frame">record</a>).</p><p><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/YuLab-SMU/Rho/main/docs/figures/frame-studio.jpg" alt="The frame: projects, tasks and files; the places as tabs; the work; the Agent"></p><ul><li><strong>Projects</strong> lists the eight most recently opened projects by name, in the service&#39;s order, the one on screen marked and a dot on each one whose Agent is answering (from one bounded <code>conversation.list</code> per project every five seconds while the page is visible; the open project&#39;s own record also says when it needs a choice). Choosing one returns to where it was left in this tab; <code>+</code> and Rho open the projects list, where a project chosen opens on its Overview. A project is a folder, and the cross on its row here or in the projects list takes it off Rho&#39;s list and leaves the folder and its files as they are (<code>project.remove</code>), with Undo for fifteen seconds (<code>project.restore</code>). While an Agent answers in it, or a session, terminal or command of the person&#39;s runs in it, the service refuses with their counts and the page asks before each is stopped (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#removing-a-project">record</a>). A project whose folder is gone from disk is removed the same way, and <code>rho remove [FOLDER]</code> does it from a shell.</li><li><strong>Agent</strong> holds the project&#39;s Agents and their conversations in one place. The row opens the Agents that work here and says when one is working or needs a choice; <code>+</code> starts a new conversation, and is the one New while this list is on screen. Under it are the open project&#39;s conversations, newest first (eight, then &quot;All N&quot;, which opens every conversation with its search, usage and trash), each one line: its Agent&#39;s mark, its title (from its section of the project notes, else the Agent&#39;s title, else its first message) and how long ago it last moved, or that it is working or needs a choice. Choosing one opens its Studio with its conversation in the panel, whichever window its session began in. The conversation the panel shows is tinted, on any page. The person&#39;s own workspaces follow, with New workspace.</li><li><strong>Sources</strong> lists the project&#39;s files by kind, as described below.</li><li>At the foot, what the project is set up with: Plugins, Project settings, and the connection&#39;s state with Connection &amp; access.</li><li><strong>The tabs</strong> are the project&#39;s places: Overview, Studio, Graph, Skills, and Review while the project has review records. The Notebook, the Terminal and the project&#39;s files are views of a Studio workspace, beside each other, and have no tab or page of their own (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#the-tools-in-studio">episode</a>). The settings or another page reached from the sidebar is named after the tabs while it shows. A bar without room keeps the icons and the name of the place on screen. At the bar&#39;s end: what the Agent is doing while it works, then Notes and Agent, which open the panel beside the work.</li><li>Below 768 CSS px the sidebar is a drawer that also lists the places, the bar names only where the tab is, and the panel is a sheet over the page.</li></ul><h2 id="the-agent-s-view-of-the-sending-tab" tabindex="-1">The Agent&#39;s view of the sending tab <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-agent-s-view-of-the-sending-tab" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Agent&#39;s view of the sending tab&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each message sent from the Agent panel carries that tab&#39;s page and, in Studio, its workspace ID, observed layout version and replay position. The service freezes a bounded view summary with the message; a retry keeps that original context. Under a Studio message, the workspace name records where it was sent from. An empty workspace has no views, even when the project contains files. An Agent can delete a workspace&#39;s layout, with its views, through <code>composition.delete</code> and restore it through <code>composition.undo</code>; one holding a protected view is refused. Deletion removes the sidebar entry and moves an observing tab to another workspace, or Overview if none remains. Project files, unsaved buffers and sessions are separate. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/studio-context-episode">Studio context episode</a> records the original misunderstanding, the real OpenCode run and the limits of this summary.</p><h2 id="starting-from-the-work" tabindex="-1">Starting from the work <a class="header-anchor" href="#starting-from-the-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Starting from the work&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A person needs to find a project, work on its contents, ask an Agent for help and inspect what happened. The application gives those actions direct places:</p><ul><li><strong>Projects</strong> lists and searches available projects, names the folder each one works in, opens folders and creates a named project from selected source folders. It opens as one list with one primary action, and with no projects it shows what can be started instead of an empty page. A new service state also holds the study Rho ships with: the application copies it into the projects folder once, under the name it is listed with, and from then on it is an ordinary project the person reads, edits, continues or removes (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/example-project-episode">example project</a>).</li><li><strong>Overview</strong> opens with the answer board: the newest message that produced results, derived from the Graph. It shows the question and the answer, then the results by role. The figure or table the answer names leads, with the other results beside and below it. Then come the method (an outline of the script), the run that produced them with its command and output, the message&#39;s other commands (which open in the Console), the data it read and any report. A result or script the project notes describe carries that description, and the session&#39;s choices follow the results: what each is about and what was taken, with any other option the notes name, under the choice or in the same sentence, in a well below with what it gives. Each result has Ask, and selecting a passage of the answer offers Ask about this. Either one sends a question to the same conversation with what it is about (the exact kept version for a file). Questions that make no new results are listed under Asked since with their answers, and the board stays on the answer they ask about; a header link names a question being answered now. Use this answer or Needs changes (with a note, also sent to the Agent) records the person&#39;s word for the file versions that message kept, and the board names results it does not cover and files changed since. Earlier answers are chosen from a menu. Below the board come a count for each kind of step (asked, read, edited, ran, changed on disk, answered), which is also the legend and filter of the Graph&#39;s timeline below it, in at most three rows: each conversation a named piece, with the person&#39;s own work and callers outside Rho among them (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/timeline-events-episode">timeline events</a>). A project where only the person has worked shows the same strip as What you did. Without conversations, the results show as pictures, newest first: the newest at twice the size, then two or five more, with the rest in Files. A side column draws each workspace as its panes, opens the shared notes, and lists the files the Agent&#39;s tools named, most used first, each with a bar for its tool calls. Its Work progress shows Agent sessions started outside Rho and any choice waiting in a session; conversations started in Rho answer on the board. Work in the Notebook is work: a Notebook section names each session with cells (where it runs, its cells and values) and the three newest cells by their purpose, each opening the Notebook at that cell; a project with only Notebook cells is not shown as empty. A project with nothing recorded yet shows its two next steps — work with the Agent or browse its files — rather than empty sections, leading with whichever one the project&#39;s listing says has something behind it.</li><li><strong>Sources</strong> counts the project&#39;s Data, Code, Results &amp; figures and Notes from one bounded walk of its folders, by file name. A project that holds them also lists Workflows (<code>Makefile</code>, <code>Snakefile</code>, <code>.smk</code>, <code>.nf</code>, <code>.wdl</code>, <code>_targets.R</code>…), Reports (<code>.Rmd</code>, <code>.qmd</code>, <code>.Rnw</code> and the page rendered beside one, whose <code>_files</code> folder is left out, as are the versioned libraries a saved page keeps in a <code>lib</code>, <code>libs</code> or <code>site_libs</code> folder beside it), References (<code>.bib</code>, <code>.ris</code>, PDFs in a <code>refs</code> or <code>literature</code> folder) and Environment (<code>renv.lock</code>, <code>requirements.txt</code>, <code>environment.yml</code>, <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>Dockerfile</code>…); each file is in one row, while the Graph and the board keep calling a file by what it is. Variables, between Code and Results, lists what the live R and Python sessions hold, by session, each name with its type, read again (at most once a second) as cells run (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#sources-that-follow-the-project">episode</a>). A kind&#39;s row shows the kind in the person&#39;s workspace, as a view at the workspace&#39;s left edge: its files in a list (by folder for what the work starts from, newest first for results and reports, each with its size and when it last changed, a filter and Read again); for Files, an explorer of the project&#39;s folders and the source folders it works in (a folder opens in place, one level at a time; hidden files on request; a file found by name among the files the sidebar counted, the first 200 shown); for Variables, the Variables view of the live sessions. These share one pane as tabs, a fifth of the workspace wide when first placed, and widened again when another view would squeeze it under 15%. A file chosen in a list, in the explorer or in the sidebar opens as a view of the same workspace, so the list stays on screen beside it: a CSV as a table, an image, a Markdown file in the Markdown reader (its to-dos tick in place; Edit shows the editor with a formatting toolbar), an HTML file as its page (below), other text in the editor, highlighted when Rho has its language, a registered result as its exact version. A script opens right of the lists. The workspace is the person&#39;s own one on screen, else the one they last used; a conversation&#39;s Studio is not written to. The chevron at a row&#39;s end lists the kind in the sidebar instead, without changing the workspace: the top level first, then by folder, at most 40 there. A tab that cannot arrange views (one opened from a link) keeps the kind&#39;s page and a file&#39;s own page, with ← and → through the kind and Open in Studio (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#browsing-sources-from-the-sidebar">pass</a>). <code>+</code> adds a source folder.</li><li><strong>Terminal</strong> is a view of a Studio workspace, placed along its foot from a group&#39;s + or the command palette, with two forms chosen in its header (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#a-real-terminal">episode</a>). Terminal is the person&#39;s own login shell on a pseudo-terminal in the project folder, drawn with xterm.js: its prompt and colours, keys to a running program (Ctrl+C), full-screen programs, at the size of the view. The view keeps its terminal with the page, so a reload or another tab returns to the same shell and the last megabyte it printed. Closing the view hangs the shell up, a shell that ended offers a new one, and a project keeps four at once. An Agent may use it too (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#the-agent-in-the-terminal">episode</a>): it reads what the screen shows as text and types there, and the view says who last did and when; a terminal an Agent opened of its own is offered here to show, and the person&#39;s own is offered back. A terminal <a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#each-workspace-notebook-its-own-and-the-terminals-commands">records its commands</a> with who typed each (zsh, bash, fish, sh, dash and ksh93; <a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#every-shells-commands-whole-and-in-the-graph">not mksh or tcsh</a>), and keeps a shell history of its own, apart from the machine&#39;s. A terminal command&#39;s row shows the start of what it printed and reads all that is kept, and the full screen a program such as <code>less</code> left, when asked. Only the person at this machine draws one; a tab opened from a link shows Commands. It needs <code>python3</code> on macOS or Linux; on Windows the view says so and offers Commands. Commands is the record the Console page showed, also opened by the board&#39;s command count. It runs the person&#39;s own single shell commands in the project folder. Newest first, it lists each message&#39;s commands under that message in the order they ran, with the person&#39;s commands and the commands typed in the terminals (In the terminal, each naming You or the Agent) between them, and Rho&#39;s managed runs at the end. Each command leads with the Agent&#39;s own words on what it was for, over the command with the project folder read as &quot;.&quot;; without those words, it is named by what each part runs (&quot;grep → python3 analysis.py&quot;). Commands in a row that changed no file fold into one line. A row shows its time, its duration, any ending other than a clean exit, and the files that changed on disk while it ran; opening it shows the whole command and its output, which streams while it runs. A running command has Stop. Run again runs the person&#39;s command again, and puts an Agent&#39;s command into the prompt to check before Run. The board&#39;s command count opens the Console at that message. Only the person at this machine can run or stop commands; an Agent may read them (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#second-pass">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Notebook</strong> is a view of a Studio workspace, from a group&#39;s +, the command palette, Skills or the Overview (offered while the Notebook plugin is selected). Each Notebook put on a workspace, the first too (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#each-workspace-notebook-its-own-and-the-terminals-commands">episode</a>), is a notebook with R and Python sessions of its own (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#each-notebook-its-own-sessions">episode</a>): a value made in one is not in another. A workspace can hold several (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#several-notebooks">episode</a>): once a page has one, a group&#39;s + offers New notebook (Notebook 2, 3, …, renamed from its tab&#39;s menu). An Agent&#39;s cells run in the project&#39;s own sessions unless it names a notebook; they show in its conversation&#39;s Studio and, from a cell&#39;s link, in a Project notebook view. A Notebook saved before this without a notebook of its own stays the Project notebook. A notebook split beside itself or shown on another page is the same notebook. Each lists only its own cells. A script&#39;s line, a table to explore and an expression from a value&#39;s view go to the notebook the person last worked in, and Variables, Plots and Data flow show its sessions (at first the first Notebook drawn), with a choice of notebook in their header when more than one has a session. Sessions lists every notebook&#39;s sessions, by notebook. Cells read in the order they ran, drawn as a notebook draws them (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#the-notebook-as-a-notebook">record</a>): the cell&#39;s count in the gutter beside the first line of its code (<code>[3]</code>, <code>[*]</code> while it runs, red when it failed); above the code, who ran it (the Agent&#39;s name opens its conversation) and its purpose, for an Agent&#39;s cell or a named one only; the code highlighted; under it how long it took or how it ended, and when; then what it printed, streamed while it runs, with messages, warnings and errors in their place and its plots. A cell names its language only in a notebook that holds cells of both. Long code and output show their first lines. Where a session started or ended the list says so, and that values from before an end are gone. A cell is edited where it stands (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#editing-in-place-and-names">record</a>): pressing its code, or Edit over its corner, makes it a box to type in with the caret where it was pressed; Shift+Enter (or ⌘/Ctrl+Enter) runs it there, Esc leaves it as it was, and code left unchanged is a cell again once the keyboard leaves it. Run again, the other icon, runs it there too. The run is a new cell in the same place (<code>kernel.run</code> with <code>replaces</code>), with its own count; the runs it took the place of stay in the record and open under it from &quot;1 earlier run&quot;. The new cell stays at the foot, drawn as a cell, with a hint from what the session holds; under the code R or Python, and Run. Both boxes are Rho&#39;s editor (<code>codeBox</code>): the language&#39;s colours, matching and closing brackets, indentation, undo, and the names the live session offers (<code>kernel.complete</code>) once a name is three letters long, at once after <code>$</code>, <code>@</code>, <code>::</code>, a dot in Python or an open quote in a subscript, and on Tab: its values, a table&#39;s columns, a call&#39;s arguments, files, installed packages. Esc closes the list; in the new cell Esc then Tab moves on from the box. With no session to ask, or one running a cell, the values it listed last are offered. On the person&#39;s own page the Notebook is the cells and that line, and leaves the rest to the other views of the sessions (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#one-set-of-session-views">one set of views</a>): a plot of a live session is a picture in its cell, numbered as Plots counts it, that brings it forward in the Plots view, which is put on the page when it has none; a value named under a cell opens in its own view; and the session&#39;s state (&quot;R 4.5.2 · idle&quot;), in the view&#39;s own bar beside the form switch, opens Sessions, with where the session runs next to it when the project names a server. Interrupt stays at the new cell while a cell runs. A wide view centers the cells; under 840 px where the session runs moves to the new cell, which is one line there, and below 420 px the code takes its own line above R or Python and Run. Elsewhere (a conversation&#39;s Studio, and the Notebook page of a tab that may not arrange one) the Notebook keeps the sessions&#39; state, variables and latest plots beside the cells, or folded into one line above them in a view under 840 px. A conversation&#39;s Studio has a Notebook view of the sessions its Agent ran cells in, with the person&#39;s cells since the conversation began, cut at the moment shown; on the person&#39;s own pages it shows the sessions whole. The view scrolls itself and stays at the newest cell while the reader is there, also when it is shown again or resized. Only the reader&#39;s own wheel, touch, keys or scroll bar take it off the end (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#the-notebook">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Figures.</strong> An R plot built with ggplot2 (directly or by a package on it) is drawn in the cell and in the Plots view from its figure record. Pointing at a mark shows its row; click, Shift/⌘-click or drag selects, Esc clears, and the rows behind the selection are listed by layer. <strong>Ask the Agent</strong> keeps the selection and opens the Agent panel with it attached to the next message; the chip&#39;s × detaches it. Marks the Agent pointed at appear as an amber chip above the plot with its note, shown on the plot when the person has nothing selected. A plot nothing described stays a picture; one that failed says why under it (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/figures-episode">episode</a>). A ggplot a cell saved with <code>ggsave</code> is drawn the same way wherever its file is shown with the bytes the cell saved: on the answer board, in the Results &amp; figures file view and Studio&#39;s media view, and under the cell, which names the file (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/saved-figures-episode">episode</a>). Layers that draw the same rows count the selection once. Pages drawn with base graphics and ComplexHeatmaps are drawn and selected the same way; heatmap cells keep their colour while others are selected, and a selection outlines them (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/drawn-figures-episode">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Two forms.</strong> Notebook | Console switches how the same sessions read: as a notebook (above) or as a console, one transcript as R or Python print it at a terminal. Each cell is its code after the prompt (<code>&gt;</code>/<code>+</code> in R, <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code>/<code>...</code> in Python, a new statement at the prompt) and what it printed under it, messages muted, warnings amber, errors red; a cell someone else ran has a comment line with who and why (<code># Claude Code · Fit the model</code>). Plots show under their prompt, or on the person&#39;s own page as a line that brings them forward in Plots. Session starts and ends are marked. A bar above names each session&#39;s state (there, a click opens Sessions) with Interrupt, and Stop where Sessions is not the place for it, and Clear (Ctrl+L) hides earlier output from this view only. The prompt sits under the last output: Enter runs finished code and goes on to another line, indented, in unfinished code (an open bracket or string, a trailing operator, a Python block before its empty line); Shift+Enter adds a line, ⌘/Ctrl+Enter runs whatever is there, ↑/↓ bring back what the person ran, a closing bracket on an empty line steps back, Esc interrupts, and the R/Python word beside the prompt switches language, as the one under the notebook form&#39;s new cell does. A click in the transcript puts the keyboard in the prompt. A Studio view saves its form with the page (<code>props.form</code>). A Notebook placed under a script by running one of its lines starts as a console (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#two-forms-of-the-notebook">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Explore in R or Python</strong> sits in the header of a table view (<code>.csv</code>, <code>.tsv</code>, also gzipped) and of an R or Python script, on the answer board&#39;s tables and on the file page. A table opens on the person&#39;s workspace with the page&#39;s Notebook beside it, or a Notebook placed in a pane right of the table, and loads into the session of that language as a new value named from the file (<code>model_fit</code>, then <code>model_fit_2</code>; a value the session has is never replaced). The cell is the person&#39;s, named for what it loads. A script opens the same way with a line that runs it (<code>source()</code> in R, <code>exec</code> with <code>__file__</code> in Python) waiting in the Notebook to check and run, since running it again writes its outputs again. On the person&#39;s own page this happens in place; from a conversation&#39;s Studio, the Overview or the file page it opens the person&#39;s workspace. The file is read as it is on disk now, by its project path, also when the view shows an older kept version. Files of added source folders are outside the sessions&#39; folder and have no Explore (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#exploring-a-finished-answer">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>A script&#39;s lines</strong> run in the live session of their language from the editor of an R or Python script on the person&#39;s own page: ⌘/Ctrl+Enter, or Run in the script&#39;s header, sends the selection, or the whole statement the cursor is in (a call over several lines, a pipe, a function, a Python block with its decorators and <code>else</code>/<code>except</code> parts), as the person&#39;s cell named for the script and its lines (<code>analysis.R · lines 12–13</code>). The cursor goes on to the next statement; a blank or comment line runs nothing. The text runs as it is in the editor, saved or not. A page without the Notebook gets one under the script; one in another pane comes forward there, and the script keeps the keyboard. Lines run in the order they were sent (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#script-console-variables-and-plots">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Sessions</strong>, <strong>Variables</strong> and <strong>Plots</strong> are views of the live sessions of their own (<code>rho.sessions</code>, <code>rho.variables</code>, <code>rho.plots</code>), added from a group&#39;s +, an empty page or a link in another view of the sessions. Variables or Plots takes a column along the page&#39;s right side, the other above or below it there, so a script with its Notebook under it and these two make the four panes of a script, a console, an environment and plots; Sessions, Data flow and Servers are tabs beside Variables. <strong>Sessions</strong> is the R and Python processes: each one&#39;s version, state, where it runs (this machine and its name, or a place), the program, its process ID, who started it and when, its cells and waiting cells, what its values take as the session measures them, and the cell running now (which opens in the Notebook), with Interrupt, Restart and Stop (both ask first); one not running says how it ended and where the next starts, with Start. Sessions that ended earlier are listed under them. <strong>Variables</strong> is an environment pane of the session the newest cell ran in (or the one chosen): its state (opening Sessions), Find, and a table of Data (two-dimensional values: a data frame&#39;s observations and variables, a matrix&#39;s dimensions), Values (the value at a glance as the session prints it, with its length) and Functions, with type and size, sorted by name, type or size. A name opens the value; its arrow unfolds a table&#39;s columns with their first values, or a list&#39;s parts, and the cell that made it and those that read it, each opening in the Notebook. Under 440 px the type is left to the value&#39;s words. Plots shows the newest plot of the live sessions at the size of the view with the cell that drew it (Show the cell brings it forward in the Notebook), steps back through the 24 newest (arrows, or the strip below), and brings a new one forward as it is drawn, or the one a cell&#39;s picture asked for. Plots of an ended session stay with their cells in the Notebook.</li><li><strong>Data flow</strong> (<code>rho.flow</code>) shows how the values of a live session were made, as a tab beside Variables (else in the right column). It is a graph of values: the project files and folders the session read at the top, the results at the bottom (the files it wrote and the plots it drew, else the values nothing reads), and the values between in rows. Each node is titled with the meaning its <code># means:</code> comment gives (the Agent writes these above the statement that first makes the value; <code># means NAME:</code> names one of several), with the name beneath, and shows the name alone without one. A bar names the results: choosing one draws what leads to it, and the first is chosen when every result at once would put more than six values in a row. Values that lead to no result are named below the graph, and choosing one draws what leads to it. A chosen value colors what it comes from and what comes of it; its panel gives its meaning, how and where it was made, what it uses and leads to, and which results it reaches. A value can be looked at, a file opened, and the cell that made it shown in the Notebook. A narrow view (under 640 px) puts the chosen value above the graph. Its values come from the kernel&#39;s records (below), each meaning from the code of the cell that made the value, and at most 80 values are drawn at once (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/variable-flow-episode#meaning-in-the-code">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>How variables relate</strong> (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/variable-flow-episode">episode</a>). Under each Notebook cell, what it read and from which cell (&quot;Read co from R 3&quot;), and the later cells that read what it made (&quot;ratio read in R 6&quot;); a cell named there goes to that cell and marks it. A cell that makes at least three values offers &quot;How its values came about&quot;, the same graph, under it. Variables names the cell that made each value as it is now and the cells that read it. An R or Python script&#39;s editor has a Data flow switch in its header, kept with the page: the script&#39;s flow, read from the text in the editor as it is typed (nothing runs), shows beside the code, or below it in a view under 640 px. Its nodes are titled by the same comments as the session&#39;s, and the flow line says how many values have a meaning written in the code. The code marks the lines that make the chosen value, those it comes from and those it leads to, in the colors of the key above the flow; choosing a value puts the cursor on the line that makes it, and a click on a line that makes a value chooses it.</li><li><strong>Servers</strong> (<code>rho.servers</code>) shows where the live sessions run: this machine and each place, a folder on one of the person&#39;s servers, reached through serverctl when serverctl manages it and with ssh otherwise. A place shows its server and folder, how it is reached, what its last check saw (system, R and Python there, the machine&#39;s name, who checked and how long it took), its live sessions with the machine and process, and a session that lost its connection, with its process there not seen to end. &quot;New sessions R / Python&quot; marks where each language&#39;s next session starts. Add a server lists the servers serverctl manages (system, number of skills) and takes one of them or an ssh name, the folder, a name and setup lines, then checks it. A place on a server serverctl manages shows the skills bound to it (&quot;How it is used&quot;); pressing one reads it in place, without its header, with its file&#39;s path, in the form as on the card. A check that finds no R where the server has skills says they may say how to set it up. Files lists a folder there; opening a file copies it into <code>remote/&lt;place&gt;/</code> and opens the copy, and a copy whose file has changed on the server says so. The Notebook&#39;s line has a chip beside the language naming where its next cell runs; session marks, rows, the console bar, Variables and Data flow name the place, and a file named in a server session&#39;s Data flow opens in Servers at its folder there. Explore in R or Python from a copy loads its original on the server; a file only on this machine is not sent there.</li><li><strong>HTML pages</strong> (a knitted report, a saved widget, a page an Agent wrote) open as the page they make, with Page and Edit (Source) in the header over the same buffer as the editor. The page runs in a sandboxed frame of its own: its scripts run, it cannot reach Rho, and nothing loads from the web. The styles, scripts, images and fonts it names in the project are read and put into it; a note says how many web files it named and which project files could not be read. A link to a project file opens it in Rho; a link outside is named, not followed.</li><li><strong>Values</strong> of the live sessions open as views of their own (<code>rho.object</code>). A variable&#39;s name in the Notebook, and each name a cell made or changed (listed under the cell while its session is live; removed names are struck through), opens it. On the person&#39;s page it opens there: in a pane right of the Notebook when both keep 360 px, otherwise as a tab of the pane next to the Notebook&#39;s, and later values join its tabs. From a conversation&#39;s Studio it opens on the person&#39;s workspace. The view says where in the value it is (a trail of parts back to the name), its kind, class and size, the session and the cell it was read after, and the code that reaches it, which Use in Notebook puts in the Notebook&#39;s line. A table shows its facts and a page of 50 rows with each column&#39;s type and summary; a column name sorts by it, again the other way, a third time back. A model shows its formula, fit statistics and coefficients, then its printed summary and its parts, folded; a vector its facts, counts when categorical, and values; a list its parts, each opening in place; a function its code; anything else as printed. The view reads the value again when the sessions change while it is shown, and when it is shown again; hidden behind another tab it reads nothing. While a cell runs, or after the value or its session is gone, the last reading stays, dimmed, with what it is and when it was read (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#looking-at-values">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Skills</strong> (listed while the Skills plugin is selected) holds how work is done in the project: the methods the person chose, the conventions they keep and the traps its work ran into, as <code>SKILL.md</code> files in <code>.claude/skills/</code> that Claude Code loads by itself and other Agents read through Rho. The subtitle counts them and names the newest change. The list gives each skill&#39;s name, what it is for and who changed it last, or that it was written or changed outside Rho, or why Agents could not load it; removed skills fold under Removed. A skill opens on its newest change: its reason as the headline, who made it (an Agent&#39;s name opens its conversation) and when, its evidence (a Notebook cell opens the Notebook at that cell, a file opens the file, a note is quoted) and the lines it added and removed, with Undo this change (Bring it back after a removal, Put the change back after an undo). A change that writes or removes the whole file folds its text. An edit made outside Rho comes first, amber, with what changed since the last recorded version: Agents read it as it is, the next recorded change starts from it, and it is undone where it was made. Then the text as Agents read it (a first heading that repeats the name is left out), Edit the file, and the earlier changes, each opening to its evidence and lines. The skills serverctl binds to the project&#39;s servers are listed read only under From your servers. With no skills the page says what will appear and how, with two things to tell the Agent. Below 760 px of page one region shows at a time: the list, then a skill with All skills to go back. The Overview&#39;s side column shows the three newest changes, each opening its skill (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/skills-episode">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>The Agent page</strong> has two lists, each with its own way in: Conversations from &quot;All N&quot; under the sidebar&#39;s Agent row and from the panel&#39;s title, Agents from that row itself; its tabs switch between them. The bar above the work names the list on show. Conversations lists every session in the project folder by day, searchable and by Agent: Rho&#39;s conversations, Claude Code and Codex sessions read from the Agents&#39; own records, and observed Harness conversations. Each row names where the session started (Terminal, VS Code, Codex app, Script…) and whether another window has it open now, and offers Continue, the terminal resume command, Markdown export and the trash (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/session-continuity-episode">session continuity</a>). Agents is one list of the project&#39;s Agents in one row design, whatever way each works here: the Agents this service can start (a radio for new conversations, the model and folded settings; one not found on this computer is greyed out), and the Agents connected from their own windows. A window sits under the Agent it belongs to, named by its MCP client&#39;s own name (<code>clientInfo</code>) or else by the session name the Agent chose; each window shows its name, a waiting, verified, refused or ended state, what its grant allows and its last call. Marks and Agent names come from the Agent icons plugin, by the words of the name an Agent gave; a name it does not know shows its initial. Below the list, one setup prompt is for the person&#39;s own local MCP client. The prompt names the project and gives one <code>rho connect</code> command, whose reply tells the Agent how to configure its own client. Reading or copying grants no access. Copy failure leaves selectable text; request failure offers retry. ACP observations and Host activity remain under a disclosure (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-connect-episode">connection episode</a>). Conversation rows retain title, model, effort, messages, state, usage, cost and their files, commands and answers; the trash restores or deletes. Runs follow when present (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-page-episode">Agents and conversations</a>).</li><li><strong>Project settings</strong> manages source folders and explicitly checks available tools.</li><li><strong>Work from other windows</strong> feeds the same Graph, Console, answer board and session Studio through <code>agent.sessions.read</code>. File links in a native answer are reported results; current bytes are not labelled historical. A continued native session is listed once. Notebook cells join its timeline by exact cell IDs in their receipts, not by assuming every call on one connection belongs to that conversation. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/external-work-episode">external work episode</a> records the actual Harness check.</li><li><strong>Studio</strong> shows how a conversation&#39;s work was done. The sidebar lists each recorded Agent session under Agent and then the person&#39;s own workspaces, marking the one shown; a conversation&#39;s Studio names its conversation in a bar above it. The <strong>project notes</strong> (<code>project-notes.md</code> at the project root) are one Markdown file shared by the person and every session, shown in the panel beside the work from Notes at the end of the tabs&#39; bar, on any page of the project. The panel holds the notes or the Agent&#39;s conversation, one at a time, so the work keeps the rest of the window. Rho appends to each session&#39;s Agent instructions that it keep one section there: a level-2 heading naming its task with a <code>&lt;!-- rho:session &lt;id&gt; --&gt;</code> marker below it, then Question, Data &amp; method, Key results with a figure, and To do as a task list. The panel reads the file formatted, opens at the shown session&#39;s section, ticks to-dos in place, and edits it as Markdown with a formatting toolbar; the person&#39;s own edits are saved after a 1.5 s pause (<code>notesTiming</code>) with the buffer&#39;s write conditions, and the Agent&#39;s edits arrive through the file. The notes are read only when the project has sessions or the panel shows them; below 768 px they are a sheet over the page. The notes take no place in a conversation&#39;s Studio layout. A conversation&#39;s Studio lays its files out by what each is to the work, as the answer board names them: the data it read above the script it wrote and a Commands view of what ran, with a Session view beside it once the Agent ran cells in a live session, figures above the tables and reports beside them, each role in one place up to its limit (<code>studioLimits</code>). A stage under 1100 px wide (<code>studioWidths</code>) has no room for two columns of readable views, so the work is laid out in two rows of its whole width: what it made, figures first, above the script, the Notebook, Commands and the data. One line above a file says what it holds and, when the work kept more than one version, which one shows, with earlier and later (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#fewer-things-at-once">fewer things at once</a>). The parts of the work sit in a chip in the Studio bar, named by the Agent&#39;s own words between steps or by what the steps did, with rounds of editing and running; time runs down the stage&#39;s right edge beside the Agent panel, long pauses folded, with the cells and commands its Agent and the person ran since it began, and a moment at one of them brings the Notebook or Commands view forward. Choosing a moment there, a part, or another version replays it: the views, the commands, the part and the conversation in the Agent panel all show the record as it stood then, to the second, and a text file shows its version kept then, read only, or says that none was kept. Arranging the views saves the layout as <code>conversation-&lt;id&gt;</code>; later files join their role&#39;s place and files the person closed stay closed. A moment replayed still shows every view the work had then, also those closed. With every view closed, Lay out the work again lays them out by role once more, and each group&#39;s + lists the work&#39;s closed views to open one there (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#closing-every-view-of-a-conversation-s-studio">closed views</a>). Studio opens on the work going on now, else the Studio last open, else the newest conversation&#39;s. The person&#39;s workspace is shared with the Agent: nothing opens there by itself. Sending a message from another page follows the turn on the Overview&#39;s board; sent from Studio, it leaves the person where they are (a new conversation started from a conversation&#39;s Studio opens its own), and the topbar&#39;s Agent chip, pressed in Studio, opens the running conversation&#39;s Studio. The tab of an open file the Agent last named says Reading, Edited, Ran…, and the topbar names the Agent&#39;s newest action on every project page. Files open from the board, the Graph and Sources (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode">canvas episode</a>, <a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#second-pass">second pass</a>, <a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#process-studio">process Studio</a>). Eight views render at once: those on screen, then the most recently shown, then the rest in layout order. A hidden view past that unmounts and mounts again from its owner when shown, keeping its buffer and undo but not its scroll or selection.</li><li><strong>Review</strong> is listed while a project has review records, since asking and deciding now happen on the answer board. It brings a frozen question, explicit Agent replies, exact result versions, keyed table differences, captured code changes and append-only human decisions together. Table row and document selection controls open the same record; whole-resource questions cover figures. The page offers the person&#39;s questions and the kept results as choices, the newest version of each result first with a count of earlier ones; any other exact version stays selectable. Sending a frozen context is a separate action from deciding. A result can focus Graph; Graph links back. Reload restores Review and its selected record. Evidence export previews that record and its retained references before writing a static offline reader (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/review-evidence-episode">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Graph</strong> says what was done in the project and lets a person open it down to each recorded step (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/graph-layers-episode">levels</a>). Closed, the project is its lines of work in the order they began: a section of the notes with its conversation, or a conversation the notes do not cover. A card carries the question, the newest figure and counts, and an arrow says that one line builds on files another made. Conversations that changed no file stand below. A line that several conversations worked on lists them on its card, and open it heads each one&#39;s messages with its title; a conversation chosen there or in the timeline shows its messages and the files it changed. Past eight lines of work the closed cards are names and counts, so that all of them read at once. An open line reads left to right: the files it used, the work, the files the work made, and what the notes conclude from them. The work opens in place, a message into its activities (looking around, cells, a script written and run in rounds, the notes, the answer) and an activity into its steps. A line to a file says whether the Agent reported it, a command named it or a change was seen on disk, and it is drawn on the deepest open node that stands for the step it was recorded on. Nothing is left out for size: what is drawn is what is open, and Close all returns to the lines. Commands typed in a terminal are steps too (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#every-shells-commands-whole-and-in-the-graph">episode</a>): an Agent&#39;s within the message it was answering, the rest under Typed in the terminal by whoever entered them, which is not a message and is not answered; a file is a command&#39;s when it changed while that command ran. Choosing a finding or choice lights the files it rests on and the work behind them, names how many recorded steps that is, and offers the step that wrote it, traced by the first kept version of the notes that holds it. Choosing a file shows the version kept, its reviews, what happened to it step by step and what is concluded from it; opening the current file is a separate action. A step shows its command, output, ending and the files as kept after it. A choice shows the option in use beside the others; Use this instead drafts a message the person edits and sends. Search covers every level and opens what it finds. Arrow keys open and close a node and move along its column. The timeline below draws time where work happened (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/graph-layers-episode#many-conversations">many conversations</a>). A conversation is a named piece from its first message to its last, and pieces that do not overlap share a row, so sixteen conversations held over five days are four rows and all in sight. A message is a band and its steps are columns stacked by kind (read, edited, ran, changed on disk, versions kept), taller where more happened, with a dot under a column that holds a failed, refused or unknown step. What the plugins record joins it: each plugin may name a read of who did what and when (Notebook cells, Console commands), and each event goes to the piece of whoever did it: You, or the conversation whose Agent did it, wherever that conversation was started. An Agent the person connected from its own window (Claude Code in a terminal, WorkBuddy) has a piece under its name for what no session&#39;s record names; the <code>rho</code> command and callers whose access is gone have the two left. Every stretch of time is wide enough for its steps before the rest is shared by duration, pauses fold to a short gap, and the axis names each day. The timeline zooms and scrolls; hovering names the conversation, the message and the steps under the pointer; a message or a conversation&#39;s name chosen there is chosen in the graph, and what is chosen in the graph is marked there. The legend counts and filters the steps, and the work replays at 1×, 2× or 4× from the axis; replay hides what came later than its cursor. While the Agent works the Graph stays and follows it: its line and message open and its flag sits on the node that stands for the step it is on. A step waiting for the person&#39;s choice turns amber and is answered there. The map of the whole shows while the person moves the view; the key to the lines opens from a button. While a message runs, each file it writes is read once it rests: its facts (a table&#39;s rows and columns, an image&#39;s size, a script&#39;s lines and functions) show at once, then a sentence from the model the conversation&#39;s Agent uses, marked with that model and the version it read. The notes&#39; words replace both when the Agent writes them (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#descriptions-while-the-work-goes-on">descriptions</a>; the first Graph and the timeline are recorded in the <a href="/Rho/docs/records/graph-episode">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>Workspaces</strong> hold real owner views in tabs and splits, which the person arranges by hand. The shown tab has a close button; any tab closes with a middle click, Delete or its menu, and a close names what it closed with Undo while the layout is as it left it. A tab&#39;s menu (right click, the context-menu key or Shift+F10) closes it or the group&#39;s other tabs, splits it right or down and maximizes its group. Each group&#39;s + puts a view into that group from the choices an empty page offers. A tab drags onto another group&#39;s tabs (a line marks the place), onto the middle of a group to join it, or within a quarter of a group&#39;s side to open a pane there; the empty strip beside a group&#39;s tabs drags the whole group the same ways, keeping its tabs. While something drags, each side of the page takes it as a column or row a third of the page. Double-clicking that strip shows the group alone in this browser tab until Restore. Tabs narrow to their icons before they scroll, the shown tab keeping its name, and a view&#39;s controls take a second row when the group has no room for both. A file&#39;s name shortens in its stem and keeps its extension; tabs short of room share it evenly, and one without room for a few letters is its icon alone. Each change is one conditional write of the saved layout, the same the commands and the Agent use, and Undo reverses it. The command palette offers split, move, close and undo for the focused view. Below 768 px nothing drags (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#arranging-views-the-person-drags-the-agent-writes">episode</a>, <a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#the-person-s-own-hands-on-the-layout">by hand</a>). A stage narrower than 640 px, as in a small window with the panel open beside it, shows a layout of several groups one view at a time with all its tabs, as a phone does; the saved layout is unchanged. New workspace, under Studio in the sidebar and in the command palette, makes an empty page of the person&#39;s own (Workspace 2, 3, …). An empty page offers the Notebook, Sessions, Variables, Plots, Data flow, Servers, the Terminal, Files and the project&#39;s files by kind; a group&#39;s + offers the same on any of the person&#39;s pages. A file opens in the view it has elsewhere in Studio, the Notebook in a pane right of what is there. The person&#39;s workspace has no bar of its own above its views. A workspace closes from the × on its sidebar row or Close this workspace in the command palette: its views go with it, so its terminals hang up, while its files, documents, sessions and conversations stay; a notice offers Undo, which brings it back with its views. Pages cannot yet be renamed, and closed ones still count toward the 32 layouts a project keeps.</li><li><strong>View headers</strong> give each view&#39;s controls one form, whichever component puts them there, also on a file&#39;s page and atop the notes: words and an icon on no ground until pointed at, the view&#39;s main action (Run) in the accent, a choice of forms (Notebook or Console, Read or Edit, Fit or 100%, R or Python) as one well with the chosen one raised, and Highlight changes as its words beside a bar in the Agent&#39;s colour. A control is there while it can act: Save while the text has unsaved edits, Review selected text and Ask the Agent with a selection, the highlight while Agent or disk edits remain (Documents 0.4.9). A file with one language has one Explore in R or Python button. The tabs give way to the controls, narrowing to their icons, down to room for the shown tab&#39;s name and every other tab&#39;s icon (168 px for one tab, 44 px more for each other); below that the controls take a row of their own, and a control that comes with a selection can then move the view (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode#toolbars">episode</a>).</li><li><strong>The Agent panel</strong> opens beside any page from the Agent button at the end of the tabs&#39; bar, from a conversation in the sidebar, or from Continue on the Agent page. Its one header is the conversation&#39;s title and Close; New conversation joins them while the sidebar&#39;s Agent list, which has its own, is not on screen. The title opens the project&#39;s conversations, the same ones the sidebar lists, to change the conversation without leaving the page; its last entry leads to the Agent page&#39;s full list. A new conversation shows only what the Agent can do here. In a tab on this machine it holds conversations with Claude Code or Codex started in Rho: follow a streaming reply and its actions, reply, stop a turn and return to it after a reload (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/conversation-episode">conversation episode</a>). Its left edge drags, or moves 24 px per arrow key, between 280 px and 760 px (at most 60% of the window); a double-click returns it to 340 px, and each browser keeps its own width. One chip under the message box names the model (Default model until one is chosen), its provider, the effort and what is switched on: the saved choices for a new conversation and the open conversation&#39;s own, changed between messages. It opens a short menu with a row per setting the Agent offers (the Agent for a new conversation, the model with its search, effort, mode); a row opens its list in place, an On/Off setting is a switch, and Escape steps back, then closes the menu, not the panel. Each ending names the model that answered (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-canvas-episode#choosing-the-agent-and-its-model">settings pass</a>). The panel renders the retained record rather than protocol events (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-panel-episode">panel pass</a>): the Agent&#39;s last words stay open as Markdown prose, lists, code with Copy and tables that scroll sideways with their numbers whole; what it did before them folds into one line (&quot;Worked · Ran 8 commands · Edited 1 file · 6 choices&quot;), open while it works. Inside, each tool step names what it did (<code>Read data.csv</code>, <code>Ran Rscript analysis.R</code>, <code>Wrote results/summary.csv</code>, <code>Called data.page</code>) with an icon for its kind and the Agent&#39;s title and reported path behind a disclosure; files are counted once; answered choices stay in the fold. Each message ends with its outcome, model, time and Copy; a failure the Agent&#39;s last words already state is shown once. A native permission request is an amber choice card. The message box is one rounded surface with the settings chip and a round Send or Stop; under it, the plan entry in progress and the context and cost the Agent reported open a card with context as a bar, the cost and the whole plan. New activity is followed only while the person is at the end; scrolling up keeps the position and offers &quot;Jump to latest&quot;. The Agent&#39;s tool status and reported path are distinct from Work progress&#39;s independently observed files. Sessions started elsewhere are listed with where to continue them. In an open workspace it also shows the Agent&#39;s recorded work and linked questions. A tab opened from a narrower link keeps a request draft for an external Agent: copying a request is not sending it or receiving a reply. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-ui-episode">live activity episode</a> records the observations, checks and limits.</li></ul><p>Connection &amp; access, at the sidebar&#39;s foot, opens a drawer: the focused document&#39;s Changes, then the connection (its state, when it was last read, Refresh or Reconnect, and why the service did not answer) and this tab (this machine or a link, its folder and expiry, what it can and cannot do, and for a link the way back to this machine&#39;s access), each as a card (<a href="/Rho/docs/records/agent-panel-episode#connection-access">episode</a>). With the sidebar hidden, the bar says the connection&#39;s state. Between 768 and 1023 CSS px the sidebar folds away while a Studio layout is open and comes back with the Overview. Narrow screens show one workspace view at a time. Closing a panel does not cancel work.</p><p>The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/home-project-episode">home and project episode</a> records the 2026-10-27 pass over these screens, its checks and the listing gap it left open. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/simulated-project-episode">simulated-project episode</a> records the 2026-10-29 run of two capsule projects and the changes it led to.</p><h2 id="state-and-code" tabindex="-1">State and code <a class="header-anchor" href="#state-and-code" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;State and code&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>web/main.tsx</code> owns navigation and session observation. <code>web/home.tsx</code> handles project entry, <code>web/overview.tsx</code> the project sections, and <code>web/plugins.tsx</code> the installed capability catalog. <code>web/shell/</code> renders the persisted composition tree and embeds registered owner views. <code>web/agents.tsx</code> handles the explicit external-Agent handoff and the session list; <code>web/conversation.tsx</code> shows a conversation started in Rho. Its presentation comes from <code>src/conversation-presentation.ts</code> (text, action lines, folded runs, plan entry), and Agent prose is rendered by <code>src/agent-markdown.ts</code> with <code>web/markdown.tsx</code>. <code>web/project-list.tsx</code> draws the sidebar&#39;s projects and <code>web/project.tsx</code> its tasks and sources; the places are <code>placeTabs</code> in <code>web/main.tsx</code>. <code>web/sources.ts</code> walks the project folders within its limits, names each file&#39;s kind and orders each kind&#39;s files for the sidebar, its page and <code>web/preview.tsx</code>, which shows one file in place. <code>web/agent-now.ts</code> reads the running turn&#39;s record (<code>conversation.read</code>, last turn) and maps its steps to project paths; <code>web/canvas.ts</code> resolves a file&#39;s owner view (<code>viewFor</code>) and places it in a workspace with one conditional <code>composition.apply</code>, through <code>document.open</code> for text and <code>agent.activity.capture</code> for a CSV or image. Following is a tab-local choice; the workspace layout is saved. Every owner view receives the shell&#39;s services (<code>ShellServices</code> in <code>src/ui-contract.ts</code>, made in <code>web/shell-services.ts</code>): a linked project file opens in place, or in the workspace on screen in Studio; an image is read as its current bytes (<code>readImage</code> in <code>web/previews.ts</code>, at most 64 per page of 8 MiB each, past which the view shows its description); <code>web/languages.ts</code> gives Python, R and Markdown to the editor and to code in rendered text, one set of token classes coloured in <code>theme.css</code>. A command the Agent ran shows how it ended (its exit code when the Agent reported one; a command that ran and failed is &quot;failed&quot;, not &quot;refused&quot;), how long the call took from its last description to its result, and the last lines it printed, opened at their end, in the panel row and the Graph&#39;s run card and details (<code>commandEnd</code>, <code>stepDuration</code> in <code>src/conversation-presentation.ts</code>). A leading <code>rtk</code> output wrapper is left out of the command&#39;s name; the row&#39;s details keep what ran. <code>src/work-graph.ts</code> builds the Graph from <code>conversation.read</code> records (a finished message keeps the project files that changed on disk while it ran) and the folder walk (<code>project.entries</code> gives each file&#39;s <code>modifiedAt</code>): questions, file versions and runs with their reported, named and observed edges (<code>ranFiles</code> tells a script a command ran from one it only looked at). <code>src/work-layers.ts</code> builds the levels on it without a DOM: the workspace, lines of work, messages, activities and steps (<code>buildWorkLayers</code>), the touches between work and files, the statements of the notes with the step that wrote each, and the reads over them (<code>trajectory</code>, <code>links</code>, <code>shownAs</code>, <code>lineFiles</code>, <code>flows</code>, <code>support</code>). <code>src/layers-layout.ts</code> places what is open (<code>layoutLayers</code>; <code>sizes.roomy</code> is the count of lines past which closed cards are brief). <code>web/work-turns.ts</code> reads every session for the Graph, <code>turnLimits.sessionsAtOnce</code> at a time and each in pages of <code>turnLimits.pageTurns</code> messages. <code>web/graph.tsx</code> draws the levels and the inspector; <code>web/timeline.tsx</code> draws the timeline, also the Overview&#39;s <code>WorkStrip</code>, as <code>src/timeline-layout.ts</code> places it without a DOM (<code>layoutTimeline</code>, <code>timelineSizes</code>). <code>web/timeline-events.ts</code> reads each plugin&#39;s <code>timeline</code> read named in the catalog, and <code>src/timeline-events.ts</code> places its events in lanes by principal. <code>src/answer-board.ts</code> derives the Overview&#39;s answer board from the same graph: <code>answerBoard</code> gives each file and command of a message a role, <code>boardThreads</code> keeps a later question that made no results with the answer it asks about, and <code>src/asking.ts</code> writes and reads back what a question was asked about. <code>web/answer-board.tsx</code> draws the board and records decisions (<code>conversation.decide</code>); <code>web/console.tsx</code> draws the Console from the same graph, with the person&#39;s command line and records from the Console plugin&#39;s <code>./ui</code> (<code>useCommands</code>, <code>useCommandLine</code>, <code>CommandLine</code>, <code>CommandRecord</code>); the Agent&#39;s commands and the managed runs stay Rho&#39;s. The live sessions&#39; views come from the Kernel plugin&#39;s <code>./ui</code> (Rho-plugins, Notebook 0.4.0): the Notebook, Variables, Plots, Data flow, Servers and a value&#39;s view (<code>rho.notebook</code>, <code>rho.variables</code>, <code>rho.plots</code>, <code>rho.flow</code>, <code>rho.servers</code>, <code>rho.object</code> at version 1), registered in <code>ui-entries.json</code> and drawn through the registry like the other plugin views. <code>web/entries/kernel.tsx</code> hands them the page&#39;s services from <code>web/shell/workspace-views.ts</code>: opening a value or a server&#39;s file as a view, code waiting for the Notebook&#39;s line, saving the Notebook&#39;s form with the page, which views the page has, and a conversation&#39;s share of the sessions. Highlighting and Markdown come from the shell services (<code>highlight</code>, <code>markdown</code>). The plugin&#39;s <code>./code</code> finds the statement a script line runs (<code>statementAt</code>), writes the code that loads a table or runs a script, what each cell read from which cell (<code>cellRelations</code>), and Data flow&#39;s graph of values from a session (<code>sessionGraph</code>) or a script&#39;s text (<code>scriptGraph</code>); <code>web/entries/documents.tsx</code> sends a line through the page&#39;s <code>runCode</code> shell service, draws a script&#39;s graph with the plugin&#39;s <code>ScriptFlowView</code>, keeps the chosen value (from the graph or the cursor&#39;s line, <code>graphNodeAt</code>) and marks the lines behind and in front of it (<code>graphMarks</code>). The HTML page view is <code>html.reader</code> from Rho-plugins (HTML 0.1.0); Rho gives it <code>pageFrame</code>, the service&#39;s <code>/frame.html</code> (sandboxed, with its own policy and no network; the page&#39;s policy adds only <code>frame-src &#39;self&#39;</code>), and <code>fileBytes</code>, a file snapshot&#39;s bytes (<code>readFile</code> in <code>web/previews.ts</code>). <code>web/skills.tsx</code> draws the Skills page from <code>skills.list</code> and <code>skills.read</code>; <code>web/skills-work.tsx</code> holds the list read, the authors and the Overview&#39;s Skills section, and hands a chosen skill to the page. The Notebook&#39;s views share one <code>kernel.read</code>; inside a conversation&#39;s Studio the Notebook is scoped to the conversation&#39;s principal, its start and the moment shown. <code>web/explore.ts</code> places the Notebook (<code>addBeside</code> in <code>src/shell-layout.ts</code>), values (<code>addWithRoom</code>) and Variables and Plots (<code>addAtEdge</code>), loads a table and makes the person&#39;s pages; the Kernel plugin&#39;s <code>./code</code> writes the code; <code>web/page-add.tsx</code> lists what a page can add. A conversation&#39;s Studio (<code>web/studio-process.tsx</code>) reads its record, builds the graph whole and as cut at the moment shown (<code>turnsAt</code> in <code>src/replay.ts</code>), takes each file&#39;s role and kept versions from the whole record (<code>studioFiles</code> in <code>src/studio-layout.ts</code>) and lays the files out (<code>studioTree</code>, <code>studioMerge</code> over a saved arrangement). <code>src/project-notes.ts</code> reads the notes&#39; sections and session markers (<code>notesSections</code>, <code>sessionTitles</code>, bounded by <code>notesLimits</code>) and writes the instructions a session is given; <code>web/project-notes.tsx</code> keeps the notes&#39; one buffer open for the project, saves the person&#39;s edits and draws the panel. <code>src/notes-meaning.ts</code> reads each section into statements with the files they link (<code>notesMeaning</code>, <code>fileDescriptions</code>) and traces each to the kept notes versions (<code>traceStatement</code>); <code>web/meaning.ts</code> reads every kept notes version, <code>meaningLimits.versionsAtOnce</code> at a time, and <code>web/choices.tsx</code> draws a choice&#39;s options. <code>src/file-describer.ts</code> reads a file&#39;s facts (<code>fileFacts</code>, <code>describeLimits</code>) and chooses what a file holds as shown (<code>holdsOf</code>); <code>Conversations</code> checks the project while a message runs and asks the Agent component&#39;s <code>describeFile</code> for the sentence. A figure goes as an image when the Agent component&#39;s <code>./models</code> finds the model on models.dev taking images, else <code>figureContext</code> gathers what made it. <code>web/holds.tsx</code> draws the line with its source and the cost of its call. The session popover in <code>web/conversation.tsx</code> holds the person&#39;s switch for describing (<code>describeFiles</code> in <code>web/agent-settings.tsx</code>) and the conversation&#39;s spend. <code>src/studio-chapters.ts</code> names the parts of a message. The views find their file and moment through <code>web/process-context.ts</code>; <code>web/shell/process-views.tsx</code> frames a file&#39;s versions and <code>CommandPane</code> in <code>web/console.tsx</code> shows the conversation&#39;s commands. For the Graph, <code>foldVersions</code> merges a message&#39;s versions of one file and folds its unrelated commands (<code>foldLimits</code>); the <code>WorkStrip</code> keeps every step. <code>web/previews.ts</code> reads the card and Overview previews from a captured snapshot, or a registered result at its exact version: at most 16 files, two at a time, figures up to 2 MiB and tables to 3 rows by 4 columns. <code>web/work-progress.tsx</code> reads <code>project.work.read</code> for the local person&#39;s project Overview: Agent-reported plans/usage, retained native source identities and current referenced files. Under the answer board it shows a conversation started in Rho only while it waits for a choice (without the board, also while it answers), and links to the Agent page for the finished ones; references the observation left out are counted under the file list. <code>web/agent-manage.tsx</code> draws the Agent page&#39;s tabs from the conversation summaries in the snapshot and <code>conversation.list</code> for the trash; an opened row reads its latest four messages through <code>web/agent-work.tsx</code>, the same step model as the panel. <code>web/agent-settings.tsx</code> keeps one copy of <code>conversation.settings.read</code> per tab for the Agents tab and the panel&#39;s settings menu (<code>web/settings-menu.tsx</code>); asking an Agent what it offers (<code>conversation.settings.check</code>) is always the person&#39;s click. The Agents tab adds an ACP Agent (<code>conversation.agents.add</code>) when the person clicks Add on one found installed, types its command, or clicks Use in Rho on an Agent connected from its own window whose command Rho knows; an added Agent&#39;s settings show its command and Remove from Rho. An Agent that offers no settings says so in the message box rather than offering to ask. Shared notes opens <code>project-notes.md</code> in the existing Documents owner. A failed work read keeps the previous view and offers Refresh. The cursor invalidates the read model; individual sources and resources retain their own versions. See the <a href="/Rho/docs/records/acp-episode#shared-workspace-loop">observed loop</a>. Work progress shows one card per conversation of an Agent, whichever way Rho came to know it: the conversation in the Agent panel, the session the Agent keeps in its own files, the same session seen while another window runs it, and its activity kept by the Host are one card. A card names its Agent and its state, shows the plan, usage and latest reply Rho has, and opens the conversation in the Agent panel; none says where the session began. A permission choice waiting in another window is said to wait there, and the Agent panel continues such a session once no other window holds it. The visible Overview rereads external projections every 1.5 seconds; it does not start a collector, Agent or Host. Turn cancellation keeps unfinished tool outcomes unknown and does not claim all native processes exited.</p><p>The browser calls the existing <code>BrowserClient</code> contract. Document content, undo, conditional save, execution identity, output and versions remain with their existing owners. Views of the same document do not acquire independent document buffers. Opening exact results uses both resource identity and version. Reconnect only reads.</p><p>A linked result initially opens as a local view. When the person arranges it, Rho includes that exact descriptor in the same conditional composition write as the split or move. The original view and its selection retain their own version; reload restores the saved arrangement. No unrelated preview is saved with it. The <a href="/Rho/docs/records/base-r-episode">base-R episode</a> exercises this path with a real Agent reply.</p><p>Opening the service URL on this machine is the access: the tab starts as the local person, so there is no link to redeem, no session credential in tab storage and no first screen asking for one. A deliberately narrower link (<code>rho --pair</code>, one folder or an expiring tab) still arrives as a one-time code in the fragment; the page redeems it, keeps that credential only in this tab&#39;s session storage, and says when the link could not be used. A tab that falls back to this machine keeps its open projects, unsaved edits and running work: leaving a link forgets a credential, it does not close a view or submit anything.</p><p>A request draft is scoped to the project and to the principal using the tab in session storage. It is local preparation, not a saved project document or Agent message. Using this machine&#39;s access instead of a link forgets only that link&#39;s credential. The existing Documents editor owns acknowledged document recovery; a view change makes no new save claim.</p><p>A conversation&#39;s messages, steps and endings belong to the service&#39;s record, not to the tab. The tab keeps only an unsent message per conversation and the identity of a message being sent. When the reply to a send is lost, Retry sends that same message once; when the service refuses it, the words go back into the box. A session started elsewhere opens in the same panel with its history, its turns marked with where they were sent, and its next message continues that session. While another window holds it, the box is disabled with where it is open and a link to continue a copy; Rho reloads a session whose record changed after its own last message.</p><p>The palette and responsive dialogs use native dialog focus containment and Escape. Typography, color and layout are implemented in the three CSS files under <code>web/</code>. These are current implementation values, not an accepted design system; they change when a reviewed result calls for it. The shared CSS variable interface lets owner views use the application&#39;s colors.</p><h2 id="from-source" tabindex="-1">From source <a class="header-anchor" href="#from-source" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;From source&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>npm run dev</code> serves the browser application as <code>web/</code> is on disk, through Vite&#39;s dev server on this machine (port 5173 or the next free one; <code>--port</code>). A saved stylesheet shows in place. A saved component shows once the page has reloaded itself, at the same address. The page stands in front of a built service (<code>dist/</code>). Without folders that is the Rho already running on the folders <code>rho</code> uses, which is left running when the command ends; when none runs, one is started for the run and stopped with it. <code>--root</code> and <code>--state-dir</code> choose other folders, such as a throwaway project folder, and <code>--example off</code> leaves the shipped project out of a new state. Changes under <code>src/</code> need <code>npm run build:service</code> and a new service, and a plugin&#39;s views follow that plugin&#39;s own build.</p><p>The service still answers only its own origin. The page server passes its own page&#39;s requests on as the service&#39;s and passes any other origin on as it came, which the service refuses. A view&#39;s rendered receipt names the selected browser build, so a receipt from this page is refused. <code>test/dev.test.mjs</code> starts the command on a throwaway folder and checks the page, both service cases and the refused origin.</p><h2 id="verification" tabindex="-1">Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>npm run acceptance:frontend</code> exercises the application using disposable projects, real R output and the installed Chrome executable. It records screenshots of the project entry, overview, files, table, figure, activity, settings, plugins and Agent panel. Its assertions cover navigation, editing and undo, an unsent conversation message, the clipboard handoff in a link tab, conditional layout changes, dragged tabs, the Agent panel&#39;s width, reload, keyboard input, responsive overflow and accessible control names. The report and images go to <code>work/frontend-reset/browser/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:agent-canvas</code> drives a controlled ACP peer that reads, edits and runs project files and writes an output no step names. It checks the sidebar counts, that a message sent from Studio leaves the person&#39;s workspace as they arranged it, the edited text on disk in the open editor, that nothing else opened there, the Agent page&#39;s record of the turn and a file it names shown in place, that the Agent chip opens the conversation&#39;s Studio, marked in the sidebar&#39;s sessions, with its data, script, commands, output, parts and timeline, that a moment chosen on the timeline at the work&#39;s first read shows the views and the conversation as they were then, that the answer board is led by the figure the turn made, with the next question and its answer under it, and overflow at 1024/390. Evidence goes to <code>work/agent-canvas-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:agent-page</code> drives controlled peers for Claude Code and Codex that offer models, settings and Claude Code&#39;s own names and report a title, context and cost. It checks asking an Agent, its names, providers and models, saving the model, effort and default; the message box&#39;s chip naming them and its menu changing the effort in place, with Escape stepping back and closing the menu but not the panel; the rows with their model, effort, context and cost, search and the Agent filter; an opened row&#39;s facts and rendered table and the Markdown export; the trash with Undo, restore and delete, never while the Agent answers; and overflow at 1024/390. Evidence goes to <code>work/agent-page-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:graph</code> drives a controlled peer that runs the real Rscript for two messages. It checks the empty Graph, the Agent&#39;s flag on the activity it is in while the Graph stays and follows, the project closed to one line of work with its counts and figure, the open line from what it used through its messages to what they made, a message opened into activities and an activity into steps by key and pointer, a figure leading to the step whose run changed it with the command as it ran, Play&#39;s narration, that following opens no files in the workspace while a file the person opens goes there, and overflow at 1024/390. Set <code>RHO_RSCRIPT</code> if <code>Rscript</code> is not on the path. Evidence goes to <code>work/graph-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:sources</code> browses a project of data, scripts in two folders and figures in two output folders from the sidebar, without a model. It checks the unfolded lists and their order, a file shown in place with no layout written, ←/→, the buttons and ↑/↓, the table and editor keeping their own arrow keys, an R script&#39;s colours, Open in Studio, unfolded kinds after a reload, a Markdown note read with its image, math, table and highlighted code, following another writer&#39;s edit, Edit and Read, a link to a script, and the 390 px drawer. Evidence goes to <code>work/sources-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:agent-ui</code> drives a controlled ACP peer through the real service and Chrome; it uses no model credentials. It checks that reply text arrives in more than one visible piece, that read/command/write actions name their target while they run, that the plan line follows the entry in progress, that a native permission is shown, answered and retained in the work that folds when the message ends, that the Agent&#39;s Markdown renders, and that reading earlier lines is not interrupted by new activity. Evidence and screenshots go to <code>work/agent-ui-acceptance/</code> by default.</p><p><code>npm run acceptance:base-r</code> runs the complete selected-row question, external Agent edit, real R execution, two-version comparison and browser-disconnection episode. It requires the configured Claude Code client and never substitutes a simulated model. Its <a href="/Rho/docs/records/base-r-episode">record</a> separates fresh execution from browser-only continuation and independent aggregate checks from scientific review.</p><p>Action, object, shell, document and continuity suites exercise the existing owner behavior through the rebuilt UI. Only executed checks in <a href="/Rho/docs/reference/status">Status</a> are current evidence. Historical component and installed-package results do not accept this new frontend automatically. Native physical IME behavior and human usability require their own evidence. The frontend reset did not implement a Project Notebook owner, native desktop windows or a registry release. Functional browser evidence shows that paths work, not that the screens can be read; the <a href="/Rho/docs/records/base-r-episode#review-of-the-2026-10-25-run">base-R review</a> records where they cannot.</p><p><code>node scripts/conversation-episode.mjs</code> drives the conversation episode with the configured Claude Code. It stops only when a step cannot be performed; the reviews in its <a href="/Rho/docs/records/conversation-episode">record</a> judge what its screenshots show.</p>',36)])])}const m=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{w as __pageData,m as default};
